1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,000
They have us surrounded, but we can't see them.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:09,000
Even though they light the way and keep our industry steaming full speed ahead.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,000
Pulled from the ground or right out of thin air, they take us higher and push us further.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Now, Gaz Tech on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:39,280
Blimps. We've all seen them.

6
00:00:40,580 --> 00:00:43,140
Hovering above U.S. cities during big games,

7
00:00:43,660 --> 00:00:46,480
or quietly circling any number of outdoor spectacles.

8
00:00:48,420 --> 00:00:53,720
From the ground, a blimp's graceful turns and steady course make it appear almost weightless.

9
00:00:54,660 --> 00:00:58,700
But in reality, what keeps the over three tons of metal and fabric

10
00:00:58,701 --> 00:01:00,300
of this Saturn lightship airborne

11
00:01:00,301 --> 00:01:04,160
is one very simple and visual use of gas technology.

12
00:01:06,260 --> 00:01:07,900
We're all familiar with gas.

13
00:01:08,460 --> 00:01:11,100
What makes balloons go up, the air we breathe,

14
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:15,240
the fuel that helps us cook food, and dry clothes.

15
00:01:16,660 --> 00:01:18,840
But gas is actually a state of matter

16
00:01:18,841 --> 00:01:22,860
and totally dependent upon external forces to keep it that way.

17
00:01:23,400 --> 00:01:26,020
Whether or not something can be in a gaseous state

18
00:01:26,021 --> 00:01:29,100
It depends entirely on the conditions that it's under,

19
00:01:29,240 --> 00:01:30,500
its temperature and its pressure.

20
00:01:31,240 --> 00:01:33,340
At the centers of distant massive stars,

21
00:01:33,720 --> 00:01:35,060
iron exists as a gas.

22
00:01:35,620 --> 00:01:38,000
While on Neptune's largest moon, Triton,

23
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:41,360
nitrogen, which makes up over 70% of the air we breathe,

24
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:42,580
is frozen solid.

25
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:47,920
In our own small corner of the universe,

26
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:50,940
the relatively stable pressure and temperature here on Earth

27
00:01:50,941 --> 00:01:54,480
allows 11 elements to occur naturally in gaseous form.

28
00:01:55,220 --> 00:01:57,380
But there are many more gases out there.

29
00:01:57,800 --> 00:02:01,360
Some are composed of different combinations of elements that form molecules,

30
00:02:02,020 --> 00:02:06,060
while other substances can be turned into a gas, such as water into steam,

31
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:08,220
by adding heat or reducing pressure.

32
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:12,160
Gas has neither definite size nor definite shape.

33
00:02:12,161 --> 00:02:17,500
So if I take a gas and expand it into a confined shape, like a bottle or a glass,

34
00:02:18,020 --> 00:02:22,880
it will easily expand to fill that bottle, and it will take on the shape of that bottle.

35
00:02:23,680 --> 00:02:26,600
That's because gas is composed of numerous molecules

36
00:02:26,601 --> 00:02:28,400
moving chaotically in all directions.

37
00:02:29,360 --> 00:02:32,320
And from that chaos comes, among other things,

38
00:02:32,840 --> 00:02:33,800
quite a bit of power.

39
00:02:34,580 --> 00:02:37,560
The United States prospers and survives

40
00:02:37,561 --> 00:02:40,020
based on its use of energy,

41
00:02:40,440 --> 00:02:43,120
and predominantly that is fossil energy.

42
00:02:43,121 --> 00:02:48,440
About 94% of all the domestic energy is from fossil fuels.

43
00:02:49,100 --> 00:02:50,500
And of those fossil fuels,

44
00:02:50,501 --> 00:02:55,680
natural gas provides about a quarter of the energy.

45
00:02:55,681 --> 00:03:01,440
This is because natural gas is made up of compounds that have carbon hydrogen bonds.

46
00:03:01,441 --> 00:03:06,760
And in the presence of a high fraction of oxygen gas and at elevated temperature, those

47
00:03:06,761 --> 00:03:12,420
carbon hydrogen bonds will react with oxygen to make carbon oxygen bonds and hydrogen oxygen

48
00:03:12,421 --> 00:03:13,420
bonds.

49
00:03:13,421 --> 00:03:19,780
Simply put, natural gas is a great source of power because it burns, and very cleanly,

50
00:03:19,781 --> 00:03:24,960
producing 50% less carbon dioxide than coal and 25% less than oil.

51
00:03:26,020 --> 00:03:30,060
Composed mostly of methane, it develops in underground geologic formations

52
00:03:30,061 --> 00:03:32,160
from decomposing plant and animal matter.

53
00:03:33,160 --> 00:03:33,868
In the U.S.

54
00:03:33,869 --> 00:03:38,820
alone, the Department of Energy estimates that nearly 975 trillion cubic feet

55
00:03:38,821 --> 00:03:41,600
is trapped in subterranean reservoirs.

56
00:03:41,601 --> 00:03:46,120
And the only way to get to it is by drilling.

57
00:03:46,121 --> 00:03:51,120
Because the easy-to-find oil and gas was exploited early,

58
00:03:51,121 --> 00:03:55,120
geologists and drilling engineers have had to go deeper and deeper

59
00:03:55,121 --> 00:04:01,120
to find additional resources, so that now the deepest gas is produced

60
00:04:01,121 --> 00:04:06,120
from over 25,000 feet, which is five miles into the earth.

61
00:04:08,120 --> 00:04:11,120
We're now on the drilling rig floor. This is the real first step

62
00:04:11,121 --> 00:04:14,120
in the operational phase of being able to drill for and develop

63
00:04:14,121 --> 00:04:16,120
and produce natural gas.

64
00:04:16,121 --> 00:04:18,120
As you can see from the drill pipe located here,

65
00:04:18,121 --> 00:04:20,120
we're actually applying the rotational energy

66
00:04:20,121 --> 00:04:22,120
through a top drive system,

67
00:04:22,121 --> 00:04:24,120
which is more of a modern technology that we have.

68
00:04:24,121 --> 00:04:27,120
This allows us to drill more efficiently and more quickly.

69
00:04:27,121 --> 00:04:29,120
By utilizing a top drive system,

70
00:04:29,121 --> 00:04:31,120
we can vary the speed at which we want to rotate the pipe

71
00:04:31,121 --> 00:04:34,120
and actually hoist the pipe up and down

72
00:04:34,121 --> 00:04:36,120
in a more efficient fashion.

73
00:04:38,120 --> 00:04:40,120
Into the spinning pipe,

74
00:04:40,121 --> 00:04:42,120
the drillers pump a specially formulated mud,

75
00:04:42,121 --> 00:04:46,000
mud, meant to lift pulverized rock and other debris out of the hole.

76
00:04:48,480 --> 00:04:53,860
This six-inch diameter pipe is already 8,500 feet below the surface and is about to take

77
00:04:53,861 --> 00:04:55,220
a turn for the better.

78
00:04:56,160 --> 00:04:58,120
This particular well is a horizontal well.

79
00:04:58,240 --> 00:05:01,440
The way that we're able to do that is we actually drill down to a certain predetermined

80
00:05:01,441 --> 00:05:05,520
kickoff point and put some tools into the ground which allow us to build an arc and

81
00:05:05,521 --> 00:05:07,280
actually go out into the horizontal plane.

82
00:05:07,281 --> 00:05:13,360
In a case like this, we'll actually drill a lateral out anywhere from 2,500 to 3,500 feet from the surface location.

83
00:05:13,900 --> 00:05:17,080
In some cases, we'll actually drill at higher than 90 degrees.

84
00:05:17,160 --> 00:05:18,380
We'll drill up a little bit.

85
00:05:18,620 --> 00:05:22,060
That will allow us over time, as we produce fluids into that lateral section,

86
00:05:22,340 --> 00:05:24,960
we'll allow those to gravity feed into the sump of the wellbore,

87
00:05:25,220 --> 00:05:29,820
more effectively get those out of the well, and then ultimately increase the productivity of the gas.

88
00:05:31,640 --> 00:05:34,960
But even with the guidance computers inside the rig's control room,

89
00:05:34,961 --> 00:05:42,800
For petroleum engineers, trying to find a gas pocket two miles underground with a six-inch diameter pipe takes skill.

90
00:05:43,080 --> 00:05:52,920
This is very similar to trying to hit a dot on the sidewalk using a very long strand of spaghetti dangling out of the top of the Empire State Building.

91
00:05:54,260 --> 00:06:00,080
The business end of the drill rig is a three-headed carbide steel bit that grinds its way through the rock.

92
00:06:00,200 --> 00:06:01,859
This is a traditional tricone drilling bit.

93
00:06:01,860 --> 00:06:03,440
As you can see, the cone's on the bottom.

94
00:06:03,441 --> 00:06:06,200
This is where we're actually able to break up the rock.

95
00:06:06,660 --> 00:06:10,200
We have drilling mud that's being pumped down through the drill pipe, comes out through the ports.

96
00:06:12,660 --> 00:06:16,960
But simply punching a hole into the gas pocket doesn't guarantee a successful well.

97
00:06:18,780 --> 00:06:24,720
Rig workers rely on a topside injection process, known as a frack job, to coax the gas from the ground.

98
00:06:26,380 --> 00:06:30,860
Over a mile below the ground is a formation that has the consistency of sidewalk cement.

99
00:06:30,861 --> 00:06:34,860
It's a gas-bearing formation, but in order to be able to produce the gas out of there

100
00:06:34,861 --> 00:06:38,560
effectively, we pump what we call a frack job, where we literally are fracturing the

101
00:06:38,561 --> 00:06:39,560
formation.

102
00:06:39,561 --> 00:06:44,140
We're injecting fluid at a high rate of speed into the formation along with some sand.

103
00:06:44,141 --> 00:06:48,300
The sand goes out inside the fracture and causes a flow path back into the wellbore

104
00:06:48,301 --> 00:06:50,720
to more effectively produce the gas.

105
00:06:50,721 --> 00:06:53,420
We do that by taking gel, pumping it into the formation.

106
00:06:53,421 --> 00:06:57,120
As you can see, it's relatively thick and has a lot of consistency to it.

107
00:06:57,121 --> 00:06:59,020
And we add into the sand.

108
00:06:59,021 --> 00:07:02,700
This actually sits into the formation, stays there, and allows a mechanism or a medium

109
00:07:02,701 --> 00:07:04,360
for the gas to flow into the wellbore.

110
00:07:06,060 --> 00:07:10,900
What comes out of the ground is usually impure and must be processed before it can be used.

111
00:07:11,400 --> 00:07:15,800
As far as the gas coming from the wells, we have thousands of miles of gas pipelines,

112
00:07:16,120 --> 00:07:21,560
anywhere from 2 inches on up to 24 inches, that gather the gas and bring it to the central facility.

113
00:07:23,560 --> 00:07:26,400
I kind of describe it like the spokes of a wheel,

114
00:07:26,401 --> 00:07:28,720
and the plant sitting in the middle of that wheel.

115
00:07:35,100 --> 00:07:38,780
The first step for producers is to remove the groundwater in the gas.

116
00:07:39,500 --> 00:07:41,600
By putting the product in simple storage tanks,

117
00:07:41,601 --> 00:07:45,380
they allow the gas to rise while the heavier liquid sinks to the bottom.

118
00:07:47,900 --> 00:07:51,000
Raw natural gas is actually a combination of fossil fuels.

119
00:07:52,060 --> 00:07:56,380
The next step for producers involves separating and purifying each of the gases.

120
00:07:56,400 --> 00:08:03,780
Many complicated methods of separation from pressure and temperature manipulation to chemical

121
00:08:03,781 --> 00:08:06,573
are used, depending on the gas's composition.

122
00:08:06,574 --> 00:08:09,180
Here at the Devon Energy Bridgeport plant,

123
00:08:09,181 --> 00:08:13,140
outside of Fort Worth, Texas, the preferred method is cryogenic cooling.

124
00:08:13,141 --> 00:08:22,500
Inside of this 140-foot tall spire called the demethanizer tower, the gas is about to become

125
00:08:22,501 --> 00:08:23,201
a liquid.

126
00:08:23,202 --> 00:08:27,780
Okay, in the demethanizer tower, we basically have the gas stream flowing up through it.

127
00:08:28,760 --> 00:08:32,860
At the right temperature, those molecules start gathering together and forming droplets

128
00:08:32,861 --> 00:08:34,140
at a minus 170 degrees.

129
00:08:34,141 --> 00:08:37,700
Just like rain, they start falling to the bottom of that vessel.

130
00:08:40,560 --> 00:08:45,640
The liquids that form, such as propane and butane, move to these tanks for storage.

131
00:08:45,641 --> 00:08:49,700
They represent a very small portion of our business, approximately 15%.

132
00:08:49,701 --> 00:08:55,600
The remaining 1.6 million gallons that we produce are sold down pipeline to the nation where they are distributed.

133
00:08:56,380 --> 00:09:04,560
The other 530 million cubic feet per day that we bring in as natural gas is sold as methane to the residential and industrial users throughout the area.

134
00:09:05,600 --> 00:09:10,160
But getting the gas out of the ground and into a tank is only a small part of the story.

135
00:09:10,161 --> 00:09:19,860
Throughout history, a long line of evolving technology has made this one-time explosive oddity into the fuel of tomorrow.

136
00:09:22,500 --> 00:09:27,440
Over 90% of the natural gas used in the United States is produced domestically.

137
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:34,500
Today, natural gas supplies the United States with over 20% of its energy.

138
00:09:34,501 --> 00:09:39,200
1.1 million miles of pipeline crisscrossed the country,

139
00:09:39,780 --> 00:09:43,280
making the use of natural gas as easy as turning the knob on your stone.

140
00:09:46,160 --> 00:09:49,360
The exploration, drilling and processing of this fossil fuel

141
00:09:49,361 --> 00:09:53,440
into a clean burning source of power has helped mold the modern world.

142
00:09:57,120 --> 00:10:01,960
But natural gas has been impacting human life for as long as we have walked the earth.

143
00:10:01,961 --> 00:10:07,180
Probably all through history, natural gas has seeped to the surface,

144
00:10:07,181 --> 00:10:10,400
and when it does, it's likely to be ignited.

145
00:10:10,680 --> 00:10:16,980
Supposedly, the Oracle of Delphi was, in fact, consulting with a natural gas flame.

146
00:10:17,160 --> 00:10:21,240
And it's possible that the burning bush that's mentioned in the Bible

147
00:10:21,241 --> 00:10:24,660
was, in fact, a naturally ignited gas seep.

148
00:10:25,340 --> 00:10:28,700
But while the Western world was captivated by the natural phenomenon,

149
00:10:28,701 --> 00:10:33,280
a civilization in the east was looking for a way to put natural gas to work.

150
00:10:34,220 --> 00:10:35,700
Around the 3rd century B.C.,

151
00:10:36,220 --> 00:10:40,780
Chinese salt producers used bamboo to drill for and transport natural gas,

152
00:10:41,180 --> 00:10:44,700
which they used as fuel to evaporate their brine.

153
00:10:46,140 --> 00:10:48,160
But despite their limited success,

154
00:10:48,660 --> 00:10:52,100
the collection, transport and storage of natural gas was so difficult,

155
00:10:52,540 --> 00:10:54,360
the fuel remained almost unusable.

156
00:10:54,361 --> 00:11:01,186
From the time when the Chinese were drilling for and using natural gas in 300 B.C.

157
00:11:01,187 --> 00:11:09,260
until about 1800, there evidently was no need or interest in developing natural gas commercially.

158
00:11:09,880 --> 00:11:19,560
It existed as an oddity escaping in various places from the earth, but there was no use for it and therefore there was no need to drill for it.

159
00:11:19,561 --> 00:11:26,600
But during the 1850s, another more manageable form of gas opened the public's eyes to the potential new power source.

160
00:11:28,280 --> 00:11:31,800
Manufacturers used a product known as coke to fuel their furnaces.

161
00:11:33,240 --> 00:11:35,900
Coke was made by cooking coal in an airtight container.

162
00:11:36,760 --> 00:11:39,580
The final product burned hotter and more efficiently than coal.

163
00:11:40,640 --> 00:11:43,920
One of the byproducts of the cooking process was a flammable gas.

164
00:11:43,921 --> 00:11:51,460
In the early 1800s, a manufactured gas was found very valuable to light street lamps.

165
00:11:51,940 --> 00:11:55,360
However, this manufactured gas is rather polluting.

166
00:11:56,080 --> 00:12:00,080
And when the opportunity came to use natural gas, which is much cleaner,

167
00:12:00,200 --> 00:12:03,080
it was considered a very significant improvement.

168
00:12:03,820 --> 00:12:09,420
The only problem was that most of the natural gas was located far from the cities

169
00:12:09,421 --> 00:12:11,940
that had the use for it as street lighting.

170
00:12:12,580 --> 00:12:16,580
Petroleum companies could easily load their liquid products onto trains or trucks,

171
00:12:16,780 --> 00:12:19,560
but that didn't work for invisible odorless gas.

172
00:12:20,200 --> 00:12:25,760
It wasn't until pipeline welding was perfected about the time of World War I

173
00:12:25,761 --> 00:12:29,740
that gas could be transferred significant distances.

174
00:12:30,300 --> 00:12:35,240
Then, of course, the economic downturns during the Depression and World War II

175
00:12:35,241 --> 00:12:38,580
meant that there was not significant demand for natural gas.

176
00:12:38,581 --> 00:12:46,620
And it was only after World War II where both the need and the technology to transport gas coincided

177
00:12:46,621 --> 00:12:49,880
that we have a significant increase in natural gas use.

178
00:12:50,500 --> 00:12:52,100
The timing was perfect.

179
00:12:52,820 --> 00:12:55,980
The consumer world was about to take a giant step into the future,

180
00:12:56,600 --> 00:12:59,660
and natural gas would provide both the power and the product.

181
00:13:00,300 --> 00:13:07,580
And it was opportunities such as plastics that developed in the 50s that really stimulated gas use.

182
00:13:08,320 --> 00:13:11,800
Natural gas is both the fuel to run plastics factories

183
00:13:11,801 --> 00:13:15,200
and is the raw material that is used to make the plastic

184
00:13:15,201 --> 00:13:20,740
that was so significant in our lifestyle changes since the 50s.

185
00:13:21,460 --> 00:13:25,960
Between plastic and pipelines, natural gas was no longer an alternative fuel.

186
00:13:26,500 --> 00:13:30,360
It was becoming as big a part of consumers' lives as gasoline and electricity,

187
00:13:30,820 --> 00:13:32,520
and its use would continue to grow.

188
00:13:32,521 --> 00:13:39,520
Today, that growth has led to a wide range of natural gas delivery systems.

189
00:13:39,521 --> 00:13:46,520
This strange-looking tanker ship is transporting over 125,000 cubic meters of natural gas,

190
00:13:46,521 --> 00:13:50,520
which will be unloaded at a specialized terminal.

191
00:13:50,521 --> 00:13:57,520
But most of the natural gas we use arrives via a complicated array of pipelines and distribution centers

192
00:13:57,521 --> 00:14:01,520
that push the product to consumers around the country.

193
00:14:01,521 --> 00:14:05,880
Southern California Gas Company is the largest local distribution company in the United States.

194
00:14:06,420 --> 00:14:12,060
We serve approximately 20 million people through about 5.4 million meters.

195
00:14:12,780 --> 00:14:17,940
That's a service territory of about 25,000 square miles, which is most of Southern California.

196
00:14:18,520 --> 00:14:22,540
On an annual basis, we move about 1 trillion cubic feet of gas,

197
00:14:22,541 --> 00:14:26,700
which is a little less than 5% of the total gas consumed in the United States.

198
00:14:26,701 --> 00:14:32,040
The natural gas enters the system from the producer's 30-inch diameter interstate pipeline.

199
00:14:32,580 --> 00:14:35,060
Though under pressure, the natural gas moves slowly.

200
00:14:36,560 --> 00:14:41,660
For distributors, forecasting how much gas their customers will use is an important part of the job.

201
00:14:43,120 --> 00:14:46,420
We're here right now at our gas control center in Southern California.

202
00:14:46,900 --> 00:14:50,640
And at this location, we actually monitor where gas is going to come in,

203
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:54,600
and we forecast where the gas is going to be used by our customers.

204
00:14:54,601 --> 00:15:01,020
So every day we have to move the gas around our pipeline system to be sure it's in the spot that the customers need it.

205
00:15:01,440 --> 00:15:07,660
And again, with the gas moving relatively slow, we do have to forecast carefully and understand our customers' needs.

206
00:15:10,080 --> 00:15:16,540
To make sure there are no gaps in the service, distributors around the country keep a little extra natural gas on hand.

207
00:15:16,541 --> 00:15:23,260
Thanks to over 400 storage facilities nationwide, if demand picks up suddenly and the supply

208
00:15:23,261 --> 00:15:30,940
in the pipeline is insufficient, distributors can quickly draw from nearby reserves.

209
00:15:30,941 --> 00:15:36,380
This is one of the largest natural gas storage facilities in the western United States.

210
00:15:36,381 --> 00:15:42,100
What makes this site, conspicuously absent of tanks and steel pipes, so valuable is the

211
00:15:42,101 --> 00:15:46,220
geologic rock formation over 10,000 feet below the surface.

212
00:15:46,221 --> 00:15:50,780
The reason the storage reservoir is here is because two miles beneath my feet is this

213
00:15:50,781 --> 00:15:52,020
kind of rock.

214
00:15:52,021 --> 00:15:53,620
This is a porous sandstone.

215
00:15:53,621 --> 00:15:58,480
Twenty percent of this rock is porous space, just like the space between the grains of

216
00:15:58,481 --> 00:15:59,840
sand on the beach.

217
00:15:59,841 --> 00:16:04,280
And that's where we compress the gas and place it in that porosity.

218
00:16:04,281 --> 00:16:09,340
We can fit fifty cubic foot of gas into every cubic foot of the rock.

219
00:16:09,341 --> 00:16:11,220
This is an impermeable shale.

220
00:16:11,221 --> 00:16:16,980
200 foot of this shale sits on top of this sandstone and that's what seals the gas in

221
00:16:16,981 --> 00:16:22,820
place so it's there for us to deliver when we need it.

222
00:16:22,821 --> 00:16:27,100
For natural gas distributors, getting the gas back into the ground takes almost as much

223
00:16:27,101 --> 00:16:29,820
work as getting it out in the first place.

224
00:16:29,821 --> 00:16:34,560
Behind me is the main compressor building.

225
00:16:34,561 --> 00:16:39,660
We've got five 5,500 horsepower units in the building.

226
00:16:39,661 --> 00:16:44,940
Right now three of them are operating and they're injecting 125 million cubic feet of

227
00:16:44,941 --> 00:16:46,320
gas per day.

228
00:16:46,321 --> 00:16:52,020
This gas is being picked up off the pipeline at about 600 pounds per square inch pressure

229
00:16:52,021 --> 00:16:59,460
and being injected into the ground at over 3,900 pounds per square inch of pressure.

230
00:16:59,461 --> 00:17:02,800
To put things in perspective, our storage capacity is large.

231
00:17:02,801 --> 00:17:07,900
It's enough gas to serve the 20 million consumers in Southern California for about three months.

232
00:17:09,660 --> 00:17:13,580
When the natural gas is needed, it's pulled from the ground and gets a good cleaning.

233
00:17:14,420 --> 00:17:19,080
Separation tanks and cooling towers, similar to those used in the initial refining process,

234
00:17:19,720 --> 00:17:24,260
remove dirt, water, and heavier hydrocarbons that have contaminated the gas while underground.

235
00:17:26,400 --> 00:17:31,240
Once back in the pipeline, the product is odorized and continues its journey to the customer.

236
00:17:31,760 --> 00:17:35,220
Once we get near the homes, we run individual lines to each home.

237
00:17:35,800 --> 00:17:40,080
Those are very small diameter lines, usually in about a half-inch diameter.

238
00:17:40,081 --> 00:17:44,640
When the gas gets to your home, it actually is about one-third of a pound pressure

239
00:17:44,641 --> 00:17:50,720
because we have a pressure regulator at the house adjacent to the meter that you see at your home.

240
00:17:50,960 --> 00:17:54,800
This is the device that our meter reader looks at when they come around monthly.

241
00:17:57,560 --> 00:18:01,660
Surprisingly, most of the natural gas we use never comes directly to our homes.

242
00:18:01,661 --> 00:18:05,260
We bring in about 3 billion cubic feet on a daily basis.

243
00:18:05,600 --> 00:18:09,460
About 1 billion of that goes to residential and small commercial customers.

244
00:18:09,740 --> 00:18:11,760
The other 2 billion go to the large customers.

245
00:18:13,280 --> 00:18:16,900
And those large customers use natural gas in power plants

246
00:18:16,901 --> 00:18:20,560
to produce electricity and in all forms of manufacturing.

247
00:18:21,560 --> 00:18:24,200
Manufacturing that helps make up the world in which we live.

248
00:18:27,280 --> 00:18:30,520
In the end, the product that captivated early humans

249
00:18:30,521 --> 00:18:33,480
as it leaked from the ground, now powers our world.

250
00:18:35,260 --> 00:18:38,740
But not all gas power relies on the burning of fossil fuels.

251
00:18:39,220 --> 00:18:42,720
Some gases are able to create energy simply by coming together.

252
00:18:43,180 --> 00:18:48,140
And the simplest gas, hydrogen, may soon prove to be the most powerful of them all.

253
00:18:53,760 --> 00:18:59,460
In 2002, the United States consumed over 23 trillion cubic feet of natural gas.

254
00:18:59,461 --> 00:19:03,080
the volume of a cube almost five miles on each side.

255
00:19:06,260 --> 00:19:10,360
One of humanity's earliest uses of gas technology as a power source

256
00:19:10,361 --> 00:19:12,680
came with the invention of the steam engine.

257
00:19:15,880 --> 00:19:20,440
First built in the late 1600s, its rapid evolution changed the world

258
00:19:20,441 --> 00:19:24,340
by providing the power that turned the wheels of the Industrial Revolution.

259
00:19:26,200 --> 00:19:29,400
But in these early engines, gas didn't create the power.

260
00:19:29,460 --> 00:19:36,460
Wood and coal were burned to turn liquid water into something the machine could use.

261
00:19:37,460 --> 00:19:42,460
Steam.

262
00:19:46,460 --> 00:19:52,460
Today, hydrogen, the H in H2O, is about to participate in another revolution.

263
00:19:52,461 --> 00:19:58,460
And even though it's the simplest chemical element, and the lightest, most common gas in the universe,

264
00:19:58,461 --> 00:20:00,460
about to turn the world upside down.

265
00:20:02,720 --> 00:20:04,860
The credit for the discovery of hydrogen

266
00:20:04,861 --> 00:20:09,020
usually goes to Henry Cavendish in 1766,

267
00:20:09,021 --> 00:20:11,580
who described the properties of it,

268
00:20:11,960 --> 00:20:15,020
and sometimes to Antoine Lavoisier,

269
00:20:15,440 --> 00:20:19,000
who shortly thereafter actually named it hydrogen

270
00:20:19,001 --> 00:20:23,260
from hydro, meaning water, and gen, meaning generation,

271
00:20:23,420 --> 00:20:26,540
because when you take hydrogen and oxygen, it makes water.

272
00:20:28,460 --> 00:20:35,600
But engineers have learned that the two gases can also be used to generate a clean source of power in a device called a fuel cell.

273
00:20:36,860 --> 00:20:42,160
The result is a potential source of energy that uses two of the most plentiful elements on the planet,

274
00:20:42,640 --> 00:20:45,180
and whose only byproducts are heat and water.

275
00:20:45,181 --> 00:20:50,180
The invention of the fuel cell is credited to Sir William Grove in 1839.

276
00:20:51,400 --> 00:20:54,660
So it's, you know, 165 years ago.

277
00:20:54,661 --> 00:21:07,933
And there never was for a century, really, an application of it that could be found that was sufficient value to the marketplace that you could call it a commercial

278
00:21:07,934 --> 00:21:08,634
product.

279
00:21:08,635 --> 00:21:20,140
I think it was really not until the 1960s that General Electric was able to develop fuel cells that NASA used in the Gemini missions,

280
00:21:20,360 --> 00:21:25,380
and then United Technologies Corporation developed a fuel cell for use in the Apollo missions.

281
00:21:26,060 --> 00:21:33,380
Though launched into space atop a pillar of burning gas, once aloft, the space capsules needed power of a different type.

282
00:21:33,381 --> 00:21:37,380
On-board computers and life support systems needed electricity,

283
00:21:37,381 --> 00:21:41,380
but the traditional means of generating power involved combustion,

284
00:21:41,381 --> 00:21:45,380
an impractical and potentially dangerous option in space.

285
00:21:45,381 --> 00:21:49,380
Fuel cells seemed ideal, and they had additional benefits.

286
00:21:49,381 --> 00:21:53,380
Hydrogen combined with fuel cells were very attractive to NASA

287
00:21:53,381 --> 00:21:55,380
because you could generate electricity very efficiently,

288
00:21:55,381 --> 00:21:58,380
and obviously spacecraft need a lot of electricity,

289
00:21:58,381 --> 00:22:02,380
but you could also generate water, and that could be drunk by the astronauts,

290
00:22:02,381 --> 00:22:06,860
And that was also kind of two for one and made hydrogen fuel cell combination very attractive.

291
00:22:09,960 --> 00:22:13,020
Today, car manufacturers are following NASA's lead.

292
00:22:13,780 --> 00:22:18,600
Since the early 1990s, automakers have steadily improved hydrogen cell technology.

293
00:22:20,420 --> 00:22:25,220
Modern fuel cells are composed of a positively charged electrode called a cathode

294
00:22:25,221 --> 00:22:30,860
and a negatively charged electrode called an anode, separated by a thin plastic membrane.

295
00:22:31,640 --> 00:22:34,660
When a hydrogen atom, composed of an electron and a proton,

296
00:22:35,340 --> 00:22:37,440
passes over the anode, it separates.

297
00:22:38,700 --> 00:22:41,280
The proton passes through the plastic membrane,

298
00:22:41,780 --> 00:22:46,660
but the electron cannot, and is sent as electricity through a circuit as a source of power,

299
00:22:47,120 --> 00:22:48,580
eventually reaching the cathode.

300
00:22:49,280 --> 00:22:52,960
Once there, the electrons and protons combine with oxygen gas.

301
00:22:53,640 --> 00:22:57,960
Ultimately, the fuel cell offers a power source with only heat and water as byproducts.

302
00:23:00,860 --> 00:23:07,060
But while hydrogen offers promise as a fuel, there are still many technological hurdles

303
00:23:07,061 --> 00:23:08,227
to overcome.

304
00:23:08,228 --> 00:23:15,420
The gas is extremely volatile, and scientists are trying to discover a way

305
00:23:15,421 --> 00:23:22,820
to store large quantities without potentially explosive high-pressurization.

306
00:23:22,821 --> 00:23:24,280
But the biggest

307
00:23:24,281 --> 00:23:28,540
obstacle for engineers trying to create this ideal power source has less to do with the

308
00:23:28,541 --> 00:23:30,540
fuel cell than the gas itself.

309
00:23:30,541 --> 00:23:34,040
Well, the biggest problem with hydrogen production is that today,

310
00:23:34,041 --> 00:23:37,200
95% of hydrogen is made from natural gas.

311
00:23:37,201 --> 00:23:38,880
And natural gas is a fossil fuel.

312
00:23:38,881 --> 00:23:41,940
And when you turn natural gas into hydrogen,

313
00:23:41,941 --> 00:23:44,340
you release a great deal of carbon dioxide,

314
00:23:44,341 --> 00:23:47,740
which is the principal greenhouse gas that causes global warming.

315
00:23:47,741 --> 00:23:52,800
So you don't really solve our problem of dependence on fossil fuels,

316
00:23:52,801 --> 00:23:55,840
and you don't solve our global warming environmental problem

317
00:23:55,841 --> 00:23:58,380
by making hydrogen out of fossil fuels.

318
00:23:58,381 --> 00:24:05,560
The goal, the dream, is to make hydrogen out of renewable resources like wind power, solar power, and the like.

319
00:24:05,900 --> 00:24:09,700
The problem is that is a very expensive and inefficient process.

320
00:24:14,640 --> 00:24:21,900
Fuel cells work because the other gas in the cell, oxygen, is one of the most reactive substances in the universe,

321
00:24:21,901 --> 00:24:25,340
causing our cars to rust and fruit to mold,

322
00:24:25,900 --> 00:24:28,680
but also keeping us alive by reacting with our blood.

323
00:24:31,180 --> 00:24:34,780
Its properties make it one of the best-known and most used gases.

324
00:24:36,100 --> 00:24:39,900
Everything from steel production to medicine, and even rocket fuel,

325
00:24:40,520 --> 00:24:43,060
rely on oxygen's reactive molecular structure.

326
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:48,200
Oxygen was discovered as a major part of the Earth's atmosphere by Joseph Priestley,

327
00:24:48,201 --> 00:24:51,860
who did a series of experiments that he published in 1776.

328
00:24:51,900 --> 00:24:57,920
And these experiments began by toying around with burning candles in jars filled with air.

329
00:24:58,420 --> 00:25:02,560
And he quickly decided to move from candles to mice for reasons that escape me.

330
00:25:02,900 --> 00:25:09,240
And his critical experiment that he did was to put a mouse inside of a jar full of normal air

331
00:25:09,241 --> 00:25:13,320
and to note that the mouse would stay alive and active for 15 minutes.

332
00:25:14,040 --> 00:25:17,220
He then produced a jar of oxygen gas.

333
00:25:17,760 --> 00:25:19,960
And the mouse lived for an hour.

334
00:25:20,240 --> 00:25:23,140
So noting that the mouse lived one-fourth as long,

335
00:25:23,300 --> 00:25:27,540
he suggested that air is made up of about a quarter of O2 gas,

336
00:25:27,580 --> 00:25:28,940
and that's close to the correct amount.

337
00:25:29,300 --> 00:25:30,600
And by the way, the mouse lived.

338
00:25:30,900 --> 00:25:33,580
He claims that he took the mouse out of the jar at the end of the hour

339
00:25:33,581 --> 00:25:35,860
and warmed it by the fire, and that it survived.

340
00:25:39,380 --> 00:25:43,100
Within a hundred years, industrialists such as steel producers

341
00:25:43,101 --> 00:25:46,360
began to recognize the advantage of oxygen's reactive nature.

342
00:25:47,020 --> 00:25:49,240
Pumping air through molten metal makes it stronger.

343
00:25:49,241 --> 00:25:53,340
by causing the impurities to oxidize and separate out as fumes,

344
00:25:53,880 --> 00:25:56,300
or as a crusty material called slag.

345
00:25:56,860 --> 00:25:59,920
For steelmakers, pumping pure oxygen was even more beneficial.

346
00:26:00,980 --> 00:26:04,640
With demand growing, scientists around the world began looking for ways

347
00:26:04,641 --> 00:26:07,380
to pull mass quantities of the gas right out of the air.

348
00:26:10,780 --> 00:26:14,360
Today, that process has been perfected by air gas distributors,

349
00:26:14,780 --> 00:26:17,520
such as the British Oxygen Company, or BOC.

350
00:26:19,240 --> 00:26:25,920
With 43,000 employees in over 50 countries and nearly $7 billion in annual sales, BOC

351
00:26:25,921 --> 00:26:29,040
is one of the largest suppliers of industrial gases in the world.

352
00:26:33,100 --> 00:26:39,000
Here at the BOC processing plant in Braddock, Pennsylvania, nearly 58 million cubic feet

353
00:26:39,001 --> 00:26:40,640
of gas are produced every day.

354
00:26:42,140 --> 00:26:46,960
The plant's production can be summed up in two words, clean and cold.

355
00:26:46,961 --> 00:26:53,960
The challenge for the plant operators is to bring the temperature of the ambient air down to the point at which it will liquefy.

356
00:26:53,961 --> 00:26:58,960
Our process starts at the air filter, which is the corrugated wall behind me.

357
00:26:58,961 --> 00:27:03,960
Inside there are some filter elements which will filter out the dust and the dirt.

358
00:27:05,960 --> 00:27:10,960
From the filter housing, it enters into the compressor via the corrugated pipe.

359
00:27:10,961 --> 00:27:14,260
The compressor has a 33,000 horsepower motor on it.

360
00:27:14,580 --> 00:27:16,700
It'll flow up to 12 million cubic foot an hour.

361
00:27:18,160 --> 00:27:20,600
The compressors push the air through the system,

362
00:27:21,180 --> 00:27:23,540
but compressing air causes it to get hotter.

363
00:27:30,100 --> 00:27:32,340
Once we compress all the air and the gas,

364
00:27:32,700 --> 00:27:34,020
we have to cool it back down.

365
00:27:34,600 --> 00:27:36,560
That's the basic function of this piece of equipment.

366
00:27:37,020 --> 00:27:40,580
We circulate water through the system at 22,000 gallons a minute,

367
00:27:40,581 --> 00:27:44,440
to our compressors, which picks up the heat out of the air and the product.

368
00:27:47,440 --> 00:27:49,780
We return the water back to the top of this tower,

369
00:27:50,320 --> 00:27:52,820
which sprays out over packing to disperse the water.

370
00:27:53,480 --> 00:27:55,640
We induce the draft up through with fans,

371
00:27:56,120 --> 00:27:59,120
and just that contact and the evaporation of the water

372
00:27:59,121 --> 00:28:00,760
cools the water back down again.

373
00:28:04,260 --> 00:28:06,700
The air, or unrefined product,

374
00:28:06,701 --> 00:28:10,000
then heads to a cooling tower to further lower the temperature

375
00:28:10,001 --> 00:28:13,920
through evaporation.

376
00:28:13,921 --> 00:28:18,040
The silver tire behind me is used to cool the air after it leaves the compressor.

377
00:28:18,041 --> 00:28:22,460
Air leaving at approximately 170 degrees Fahrenheit enters the tire in the base, water

378
00:28:22,461 --> 00:28:27,280
is sprayed down over top of it, and the air comes out at the top of the tire at approximately

379
00:28:27,281 --> 00:28:31,620
47 degrees Fahrenheit.

380
00:28:31,621 --> 00:28:37,000
The air then passes through a chemical process to remove CO2 and water vapor, both of which,

381
00:28:37,001 --> 00:28:41,670
left inside the airstream would freeze, plugging the machine.

382
00:28:41,671 --> 00:28:44,120
The air then enters an expansion

383
00:28:44,121 --> 00:28:47,128
turbine, where most of the cooling occurs.

384
00:28:47,129 --> 00:28:50,280
Just as an aerosol can becomes cold when its

385
00:28:50,281 --> 00:28:55,840
pressurized contents are released, the expansion turbine, as its name implies, allows the gas

386
00:28:55,841 --> 00:29:01,606
to expand, which reduces its pressure, causing it to shed heat.

387
00:29:01,607 --> 00:29:04,260
The final step in the process

388
00:29:04,261 --> 00:29:07,320
involves distilling the gas into its individual elements.

389
00:29:08,720 --> 00:29:13,140
The 170-foot tire behind me is where the actual distillation occurs.

390
00:29:14,280 --> 00:29:17,540
As the air enters in at minus 270 degrees Fahrenheit,

391
00:29:17,860 --> 00:29:21,880
we keep chilling the air down until it hits the liquefaction point of oxygen.

392
00:29:22,720 --> 00:29:25,140
The liquid oxygen forms, gravity takes over,

393
00:29:25,980 --> 00:29:28,040
argon and nitrogen gas continues up the column.

394
00:29:28,860 --> 00:29:32,000
Argon's next, it liquefies, falls back down,

395
00:29:32,001 --> 00:29:35,000
Nitrogen continues up to the top of the column.

396
00:29:35,001 --> 00:29:40,000
From there, the separated gases travel to storage tanks.

397
00:29:40,001 --> 00:29:49,000
This BOC plant uses a pipeline to ship oxygen to its largest customer, a local steel mill.

398
00:29:49,001 --> 00:29:54,000
Trucks and train cars are used to transport to smaller customers.

399
00:29:54,001 --> 00:29:58,000
But carrying cryogenic liquid takes a very special big rig.

400
00:29:58,001 --> 00:30:01,000
The trailers we use are like a big thermos bottle.

401
00:30:01,001 --> 00:30:05,760
You have an aluminum tanker inside, and you have a steel vessel outside.

402
00:30:06,060 --> 00:30:08,540
It's filled with an insulation material called perlite.

403
00:30:08,920 --> 00:30:12,780
The product that leaves this site as a liquid is put into a truck, as you see behind me filling.

404
00:30:13,140 --> 00:30:15,620
We'll fill it up to just under 80,000 pounds.

405
00:30:15,940 --> 00:30:20,240
The driver pulls onto the scales, hooks up the hoses, he'll program his scales,

406
00:30:20,800 --> 00:30:23,400
and once everything is verified, he'll start filling.

407
00:30:24,220 --> 00:30:28,080
At a certain pre-programmed limit, the scales will shut the valves,

408
00:30:28,081 --> 00:30:31,680
and the drivers will take a final test and button the trailer up.

409
00:30:38,320 --> 00:30:41,640
But while some gases work because of their ability to react,

410
00:30:42,100 --> 00:30:44,240
others are prized for doing just the opposite.

411
00:30:44,760 --> 00:30:48,120
And the noble nature of a small number of gases such as helium,

412
00:30:48,580 --> 00:30:51,140
best known as the gas that makes blint slowly rise,

413
00:30:51,141 --> 00:30:56,100
is also responsible for the fastest and highest flying technology ever built.

414
00:30:58,080 --> 00:31:06,780
During launch, the space shuttle's three main engines consume around 60,000 gallons of liquid hydrogen and liquid oxygen every minute.

415
00:31:09,240 --> 00:31:16,500
Helium, neon, argon, krypton, xenon, and radon are the six elements known as noble gases.

416
00:31:17,060 --> 00:31:19,100
Noble because they don't react with anything.

417
00:31:20,120 --> 00:31:24,960
At the molecular level, these gases have even numbers of electrons in their outer shells.

418
00:31:24,961 --> 00:31:30,120
This means they're stable and won't try to share electrons or react with other elements.

419
00:31:32,360 --> 00:31:35,760
One of the most widely used noble gases is helium.

420
00:31:36,360 --> 00:31:39,220
From party balloons to blimps circling overhead,

421
00:31:39,600 --> 00:31:42,320
we're all familiar with helium's lighter-than-air characteristics.

422
00:31:43,100 --> 00:31:46,800
Its specific gravity is 0.138.

423
00:31:46,960 --> 00:31:52,020
Now, specific gravity is its relative weight to air, air being 1.0.

424
00:31:52,021 --> 00:31:57,900
So if you put helium in air, it will naturally rise to the top.

425
00:31:58,280 --> 00:32:01,360
And in fact, it will eventually rise and float out of the atmosphere.

426
00:32:01,720 --> 00:32:05,000
That's why there's such a small amount of helium in the atmosphere,

427
00:32:05,400 --> 00:32:08,120
is because it's continually floating and going out into space.

428
00:32:08,980 --> 00:32:13,860
Here on Earth, the gas is mainly found underground, within natural gas deposits.

429
00:32:14,560 --> 00:32:18,000
But in space, the main production centers are stars.

430
00:32:18,940 --> 00:32:24,140
Helium was first discovered by Pierre Jensen in 1868 by looking at emission specter from the sun.

431
00:32:24,141 --> 00:32:28,100
And he noted a yellow emission line in the spectrum,

432
00:32:28,520 --> 00:32:32,280
and it did not correspond to the emission line of any known element.

433
00:32:32,520 --> 00:32:36,900
And he suggested that it was emitted by an element that's unknown in the earth,

434
00:32:36,980 --> 00:32:41,640
but it was common in the sun, and he named it after the sun god Helios.

435
00:32:41,880 --> 00:32:42,580
He called it helium.

436
00:32:42,581 --> 00:32:43,799
He was laughed at.

437
00:32:43,800 --> 00:32:50,960
It was considered ridiculous to identify an element in nature without having isolated in the laboratory.

438
00:32:52,220 --> 00:33:00,060
Despite the gas's undignified discovery, within 50 years the United States government began to see helium's serious military potential.

439
00:33:02,080 --> 00:33:04,407
Near the end of the First World War, the U.S.

440
00:33:04,408 --> 00:33:09,680
commissioned a fleet of helium-filled balloons to perform reconnaissance missions against the Germans.

441
00:33:09,681 --> 00:33:15,140
The original reason the government got into the helium business was for observation balloons

442
00:33:15,141 --> 00:33:18,800
used during World War I for artillery spotting in Europe.

443
00:33:19,340 --> 00:33:22,220
The advantage to helium, of course, is that it's non-flammable.

444
00:33:22,820 --> 00:33:26,240
And you can shoot at that observation balloon, you just put a little hole in it,

445
00:33:26,320 --> 00:33:29,160
and it'll slowly come to the ground and everyone walks away.

446
00:33:29,980 --> 00:33:33,260
In the late 1920s, the U.S. government began building the Akron,

447
00:33:33,480 --> 00:33:38,040
and soon after, the Macon, held aloft by 6 million cubic feet of helium each.

448
00:33:38,820 --> 00:33:41,220
The rigid airships were the largest in U.S. history.

449
00:33:43,300 --> 00:33:46,480
Dirigibles continued their military service into the Second World War.

450
00:33:47,260 --> 00:33:52,060
During the invasion of Normandy, special helium-filled blimps called barrage balloons

451
00:33:52,061 --> 00:33:54,460
protected ships from attacks by the Luftwaffe.

452
00:33:55,260 --> 00:34:00,460
But over time, the government's real interest in helium was sparked not by how it reacted under fire,

453
00:34:00,840 --> 00:34:04,020
but how, as an industrial gas, it didn't react at all.

454
00:34:04,021 --> 00:34:11,020
Aerospace engineers in the 1950s pushed the limits of metallurgy in their quest to build

455
00:34:11,100 --> 00:34:16,940
ever faster aircraft. Both extremely light and exceptionally strong, airframes built

456
00:34:16,941 --> 00:34:21,860
from titanium opened the door to supersonic flight.

457
00:34:21,861 --> 00:34:24,835
But welding the metal together proved difficult.

458
00:34:24,836 --> 00:34:27,500
Welders who use electricity, such as in arc

459
00:34:27,501 --> 00:34:30,338
welding, must be wary of reactive gases.

460
00:34:30,339 --> 00:34:33,460
As the electricity melts the metal, gases in

461
00:34:33,461 --> 00:34:37,990
the air react to the charge, causing impurities that weaken the weld.

462
00:34:37,991 --> 00:34:39,500
By pumping non-reactive

463
00:34:39,501 --> 00:34:45,280
helium through the melting titanium, aerospace engineers in the 1950s were able to get welds

464
00:34:45,281 --> 00:34:49,200
so strong it was as if the pieces had been forged from a single sheet of metal.

465
00:34:53,000 --> 00:34:57,500
The military found other uses for helium as they constructed weapons for the Cold War.

466
00:34:57,501 --> 00:35:07,953
During the beginnings of the Cold War, helium really came of age and was used in great quantities in the Titan missile program and in the Atlas missile program as a

467
00:35:07,954 --> 00:35:13,180
purge gas and as a leak detection gas used in the fuel systems for those missiles.

468
00:35:15,720 --> 00:35:19,980
For engineers, helium is the perfect choice when it comes to looking for holes.

469
00:35:20,560 --> 00:35:25,200
The second element on the periodic chart, which denotes its minuscule atomic weight,

470
00:35:25,820 --> 00:35:27,880
helium is structurally one of the smallest atoms.

471
00:35:28,580 --> 00:35:33,380
That means it can leak out of holes that are too small for water, or even other gases to fit through.

472
00:35:34,120 --> 00:35:36,760
Sometimes it leaks right through seemingly solid material.

473
00:35:38,140 --> 00:35:42,600
Even though solid substances seem impermeable, to helium they are not.

474
00:35:42,960 --> 00:35:45,600
If we take just a regular latex balloon, fill it with helium,

475
00:35:45,601 --> 00:35:49,200
we can take this device, which is a handheld helium leak detector,

476
00:35:49,201 --> 00:35:52,160
hold its sniffer near the surface of the balloon,

477
00:35:52,260 --> 00:35:54,560
and we can detect the helium that diffuses through the balloon.

478
00:35:58,320 --> 00:36:02,660
By the late 1950s, helium had become so vital to national defense,

479
00:36:03,100 --> 00:36:05,260
the government wanted to make sure it never ran out.

480
00:36:06,060 --> 00:36:07,018
Fortunately, the U.S.

481
00:36:07,019 --> 00:36:10,120
was home to one of the richest natural helium reserves in the world,

482
00:36:10,121 --> 00:36:12,160
the Hugotin Natural Gas Field,

483
00:36:12,580 --> 00:36:17,220
located deep in the rocks below the Texas and Oklahoma panhandles and parts of Kansas.

484
00:36:17,221 --> 00:36:23,220
The Huguenin gas field was the major producer of natural gas for the United States.

485
00:36:23,221 --> 00:36:29,220
It contains about 0.6% of helium, and the government recognized that if something wasn't done quickly,

486
00:36:29,221 --> 00:36:33,220
that helium in that natural gas would be lost forever.

487
00:36:33,221 --> 00:36:40,220
And that's what prompted the Congress and the administration to get together and pass the Helium Privatization Act of 1960.

488
00:36:40,221 --> 00:36:51,300
Under Congress's urging in 1962, the Bureau of Land Management converted a depleted gas field outside of Amarillo, Texas, into the Bush Dome Reservoir.

489
00:36:52,220 --> 00:36:54,694
The Bush Dome is kind of a confusing term.

490
00:36:54,695 --> 00:36:57,462
It's not really a dome or anything you can see.

491
00:36:57,463 --> 00:37:00,820
It's a geological formation about 3,000 feet underground.

492
00:37:00,821 --> 00:37:07,360
It covers about 11,000 acres from the surface, and what it consists of is a layer that's

493
00:37:07,361 --> 00:37:12,740
about 500 to 1,000 feet thick of dolomite rock, which is a porous rock.

494
00:37:13,140 --> 00:37:17,460
The gas is actually stored in the rock like you would store water in a sponge.

495
00:37:18,180 --> 00:37:21,500
On top of the dolomite rock is the anhydrite rock.

496
00:37:21,720 --> 00:37:26,540
It's very hard, it's not porous, and it acts as a cap and is often called the cap rock.

497
00:37:26,541 --> 00:37:33,640
Over the course of nine years, the government bought the helium byproduct from public natural gas producers.

498
00:37:35,240 --> 00:37:40,940
By the early 1970s, it had entombed over 36 billion cubic feet of gas.

499
00:37:41,760 --> 00:37:43,418
This right here is a Bivens A6 well.

500
00:37:43,419 --> 00:37:45,720
It's the richest helium content well in the world.

501
00:37:46,440 --> 00:37:54,760
It runs about 3,500 feet to the bottom formation, and the natural pressure on this is probably just a little under 600 right now.

502
00:37:54,761 --> 00:37:59,620
due to the injection and withdrawal and the high content of helium now,

503
00:37:59,720 --> 00:38:01,380
it's a 72% helium well.

504
00:38:03,040 --> 00:38:07,080
But with the passing of the Cold War, the government's need for helium diminished.

505
00:38:08,100 --> 00:38:11,280
Today, it's private industry that keeps the helium trade going strong

506
00:38:11,281 --> 00:38:13,180
and the bush dome in business.

507
00:38:17,320 --> 00:38:19,800
Helium is used in many high-tech fields.

508
00:38:20,520 --> 00:38:24,020
Its extremely low boiling point makes liquid helium an ideal coolant

509
00:38:24,021 --> 00:38:25,860
for the magnets and MRI machines.

510
00:38:27,640 --> 00:38:30,260
But helium is mainly used by manufacturers

511
00:38:30,261 --> 00:38:32,640
of computer chips and fiber optic cables,

512
00:38:33,020 --> 00:38:34,640
whose success depends on keeping

513
00:38:34,641 --> 00:38:37,220
even the smallest contaminants out of their products.

514
00:38:39,420 --> 00:38:43,020
American industry uses roughly 5.5 billion cubic feet

515
00:38:43,021 --> 00:38:44,180
of helium every year.

516
00:38:46,060 --> 00:38:49,580
And the bush dome, which once paid producers top dollar

517
00:38:49,581 --> 00:38:51,940
to buy the gas, now receives payment

518
00:38:51,941 --> 00:38:54,760
from the same producers for storage space rental.

519
00:38:57,820 --> 00:39:01,840
But not all gases work their invisible magic behind the scenes,

520
00:39:02,520 --> 00:39:04,580
and some eye-popping new technology

521
00:39:04,581 --> 00:39:06,420
is pushing another group of gases

522
00:39:06,421 --> 00:39:09,080
to an electrifying future.

523
00:39:11,680 --> 00:39:13,560
To prevent deterioration,

524
00:39:14,160 --> 00:39:16,100
the Declaration of Independence is kept

525
00:39:16,101 --> 00:39:18,520
in a sealed titanium and glass container

526
00:39:18,521 --> 00:39:20,700
filled with a noble gas,

527
00:39:20,701 --> 00:39:21,401
Argon.

528
00:39:21,402 --> 00:39:31,380
Neon, mercury, helium, xenon, and krypton create the spectrum of colors we collectively

529
00:39:31,381 --> 00:39:33,341
refer to as neon lights.

530
00:39:33,342 --> 00:39:38,980
From the sacred glow of an all-night wedding chapel sign to the gaudy

531
00:39:38,981 --> 00:39:43,620
glitz of the Las Vegas Strip, these gas lights have changed the way we see the world.

532
00:39:45,920 --> 00:39:49,200
That's because electricity excites the electrons of the gas,

533
00:39:49,700 --> 00:39:53,060
causing them to give off photons, which we see as colored light.

534
00:39:54,040 --> 00:39:58,840
So the way that a neon light bulb works is by discharging electricity through neon gas.

535
00:39:59,220 --> 00:40:05,200
The neon gas begins with its outermost shell of electrons in a relatively stable or ground state.

536
00:40:05,660 --> 00:40:10,160
It's analogous to the state that this book is in when it's lying flat in my hands.

537
00:40:10,161 --> 00:40:13,880
It's difficult to move and nudge about, and it's as low as it can get.

538
00:40:14,320 --> 00:40:16,680
When the electricity passes through the gas,

539
00:40:16,880 --> 00:40:19,880
it pumps up those electrons into a higher energy state.

540
00:40:20,480 --> 00:40:22,060
And when the electricity has passed,

541
00:40:22,460 --> 00:40:25,100
the electrons fall back down to their ground state,

542
00:40:25,460 --> 00:40:28,580
and just like a sound is emitted when the book hits my hand,

543
00:40:29,020 --> 00:40:32,580
light is emitted when the electrons fall back down to their initial states.

544
00:40:34,500 --> 00:40:39,060
A plasma ball is a type of neon light that seems to capture electricity itself.

545
00:40:39,600 --> 00:40:42,000
To make one of these glowing balls come to life,

546
00:40:42,001 --> 00:40:45,900
Light makers carefully mix a number of gases.

547
00:40:45,901 --> 00:40:49,300
But first, they must make sure they start with a clean slate,

548
00:40:49,301 --> 00:40:51,300
or at least an empty globe.

549
00:40:51,301 --> 00:40:55,000
We put it on the vacuum system and pump all the air out

550
00:40:55,001 --> 00:40:57,700
and try to get it as pure as possible.

551
00:40:57,701 --> 00:41:02,600
Often we'll bake them up to maybe 700 degrees for an hour.

552
00:41:02,601 --> 00:41:05,700
Get all the impurities out because it's very important.

553
00:41:05,701 --> 00:41:09,000
Impurities can contribute a lot to the mixture.

554
00:41:09,001 --> 00:41:14,880
Then once that's been done, then we backfill into this vacuum we've created.

555
00:41:15,660 --> 00:41:22,120
At the center of the ball, a stainless steel core connected to a power source acts as a contact for the gas.

556
00:41:23,920 --> 00:41:28,220
As xenon and krypton fill the vacuum, a faint glow begins to appear.

557
00:41:28,900 --> 00:41:36,360
We put in our base mixture of xenon and krypton, and now we're backfilling it with neon gas.

558
00:41:36,361 --> 00:41:40,920
and that'll give us the body of the mixture with the arms.

559
00:41:42,200 --> 00:41:44,680
The spider-like arms that develop inside the ball

560
00:41:44,681 --> 00:41:47,240
are manipulated by altering the power input.

561
00:41:47,920 --> 00:41:49,380
This is a frequency adjust.

562
00:41:49,460 --> 00:41:52,660
I can actually tune the power supply to the globe

563
00:41:52,661 --> 00:41:57,880
and different globes will vary or different loads will vary.

564
00:41:58,800 --> 00:42:00,320
Now we have a finished globe.

565
00:42:01,020 --> 00:42:04,680
This is ready to go to a children's museum in Mexico City.

566
00:42:06,360 --> 00:42:11,060
But not all gas lights are meant to be colorful.

567
00:42:11,061 --> 00:42:13,860
Fluorescent and halogen lights are highly efficient

568
00:42:13,861 --> 00:42:16,360
and last much longer than conventional bulbs.

569
00:42:16,361 --> 00:42:20,360
Fluorescent lights work when electricity turns a small amount

570
00:42:20,361 --> 00:42:24,360
of liquid mercury into gas inside the sealed tube.

571
00:42:24,361 --> 00:42:27,360
So fluorescent light bulbs work by discharging electricity

572
00:42:27,361 --> 00:42:31,360
through a mixture of argon gas and mercury vapor.

573
00:42:31,361 --> 00:42:34,360
The electricity pumps the electrons in the outer shells

574
00:42:34,361 --> 00:42:38,860
shells of the mercury atoms into an excited state and when they cascade back

575
00:42:38,861 --> 00:42:43,780
down to their ground state they emit ultraviolet radiation. This ultraviolet

576
00:42:43,781 --> 00:42:48,820
radiation is then absorbed by a powder on the inside wall of the light bulb and

577
00:42:48,821 --> 00:42:55,240
then that compound emits visible light. Stimulating the phosphorus takes far

578
00:42:55,241 --> 00:43:00,220
less energy than in a conventional bulb. That means even though fluorescent lights

579
00:43:00,221 --> 00:43:03,580
contain hazardous mercury, they are usually considered environmentally

580
00:43:03,581 --> 00:43:09,339
friendly because they save electricity, which reduces power plant emissions.

581
00:43:09,340 --> 00:43:10,400
Halogen lights

582
00:43:10,401 --> 00:43:11,678
are even more advanced.

583
00:43:11,679 --> 00:43:15,180
In a typical light bulb, electricity passes through a filament,

584
00:43:15,420 --> 00:43:17,275
causing it to burn and glow.

585
00:43:17,276 --> 00:43:21,780
So for example, the most common form of light bulb is a little strip

586
00:43:21,781 --> 00:43:26,340
of tungsten metal that you pass an electrical current through until it heats up to the point

587
00:43:26,341 --> 00:43:28,789
that it incandesces or gives off light.

588
00:43:28,790 --> 00:43:32,620
If you were to do that in oxygen, in normal air, the tungsten

589
00:43:32,621 --> 00:43:37,148
wire would oxidize and make tungsten oxide and it would burn out in seconds.

590
00:43:37,149 --> 00:43:38,280
If you do it in the

591
00:43:38,281 --> 00:43:44,300
presence of argon, which is what fills many light bulbs, then it will be unreactive and will persist,

592
00:43:44,440 --> 00:43:49,800
won't oxidize. Thanks to the gas inside the bulb, the filament can last for years,

593
00:43:50,740 --> 00:43:55,200
allowing these lights to be economical and efficient while also offering us a future

594
00:43:55,201 --> 00:43:57,500
that is both brighter and cleaner.

595
00:43:59,860 --> 00:44:03,060
The power and preservation of this simple state of matter

596
00:44:03,061 --> 00:44:05,300
affects our lives every day.

597
00:44:05,960 --> 00:44:07,420
And whether it's lighting a room,

598
00:44:08,000 --> 00:44:10,420
or running a power plant that lights an entire city,

599
00:44:11,340 --> 00:44:15,300
gas technology has us surrounded every step of the way.

